  "response": "## Picking `dateLabel` (src/date.ts:38–42)\n\n**Function to fix first:** `dateLabel()` at lines 38–42. Both halves of the `mixed_utc_local_methods` finding (crime_00003) sit inside one tiny function — `todayKey` at L9 is consistent UTC, so it isn't part of the mix.\n\n**Receiver whose methods disagree:** `d` (the `new Date()` bound on L40). On L41 it reads `getUTCFullYear()` (UTC family) and then `getMonth()` + `getDate()` (local family) on the same instance — those silently disagree whenever the host's UTC offset crosses a year/month/day boundary.\n\n**Refactor — pick UTC throughout:**\n\n```ts\nexport function dateLabel(): string {\n  const d = new Date();\n  const year = d.getUTCFullYear();\n  const month = String(d.getUTCMonth() + 1).padStart(2, \"0\");\n  const day = String(d.getUTCDate()).padStart(2, \"0\");\n  return `${year}-${month}-${day}`;\n}\n```\n\nWhy UTC: it matches the sibling `todayKey()` immediately above, which is already on the UTC family — keeping `dateLabel` UTC means the two \"today\" strings agree at midnight UTC instead of drifting against each other across timezones. The conversion to local should only happen at a display boundary (and through `Intl.DateTimeFormat`, per the related crime_00025 finding), never mid-arithmetic.\n\nThis single change also resolves the adjacent crime_00024 (`Date String Sewing`) by zero-padding and correcting the 0-indexed month — both bugs the comment on L36 calls out.",
